WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:04.940
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:09.410
As we discussed before, simulating the motion
of fluids and smoke with a computer program

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.219
is a very expensive process.

00:00:11.219 --> 00:00:15.499
We have to compute quantities like the velocity
and the pressure of a piece of fluid at every

00:00:15.499 --> 00:00:17.200
given point in space.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:22.300
Even though we cannot compute them everywhere,
we can place a 3D grid and compute these quantities

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:27.310
in the gridpoints, and use mathematical techniques
to find out what is exactly happening between

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:28.360
these gridpoints.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:33.570
Still, even if we do this, we still have to
wait up to days, even for a few seconds of

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:34.989
video footage.

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:39.769
One possible way to alleviate this would be
to write an adaptive simulation program.

00:00:39.769 --> 00:00:43.160
Adaptive means that the simulator tries to
adapt to the problem at hand.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:48.059
Here it means that recognizes the regions
where it needs to focus lot of computational

00:00:48.059 --> 00:00:52.850
resources on, and at the same time it also
tries to find regions where it can get away

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.920
with using less computation.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:59.520
Here you can see spheres of different sizes
- in regions where there is a lot going on,

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:01.149
you will see smaller spheres.

00:01:01.149 --> 00:01:05.230
This means that we have a finer grid in this
region, therefore we know more about what

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:06.870
is exactly happening there.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:11.360
In other places, you also see larger spheres,
meaning that the resolution of our grid is

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.000
more coarse in these regions.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.870
This we can get away with only because there
is not much happening there.

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:21.430
Essentially, we focus our resources to regions
that really require it, for instance, where

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.680
there are lots of small-scale details.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.110
The spheres are only used for the sake of
visualization, the actual output of the simulator

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:33.550
looks like this.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:37.510
It also takes into consideration which regions
we're currently looking at.

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:43.110
Here we're watching one side of the corridor,
where the simulator will take this into consideration

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:47.940
and create a highly detailed simulation - at
the cost of sacrificing details on the other

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:53.630
side of the corridor, but that's fine, because
we don't see any of that.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:58.640
However, there may some objects the fluid
needs to interact with.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.500
Here, the algorithm makes sure to increase
the resolution so that particles can correctly

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.110
flow through the holes of this object.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:10.880
The authors have also published the source
code of their technique, so anyone with a

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:15.500
bit of programming knowledge can start playing
with this amazing piece of work.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.140
The world of research is incredibly fast moving.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:22.360
When you are done with something, you immediately
need to jump onto the next project.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:26.960
Two Minute Papers is a series where we slow
down a bit, and celebrate these wonderful

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:27.960
works.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:32.000
We are also trying to show that research is
not only for experts, it is for everyone.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.690
If you like this series, please make sure
to help me spread the word, and share the

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:39.440
series to your friends, so we can all marvel
at these beautiful works.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.590
Thanks for watching, and I'll see you next
time!

